WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.620
Three days before Christmas in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:11.220
76-year-old Michael Faherty died in a house&nbsp;
fire in the picturesque Irish city of Galway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.980
People die in fires all too often, but&nbsp;
this case was, shall we say, unusual.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:24.240
What was once Michael's body was now a pile&nbsp;
of ash, as though he’d been consumed by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.280
towering inferno... but that made absolutely&nbsp;
no sense. Because aside from the armchair in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.840
which his body was found, the rest of&nbsp;
his house was practically untouched.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.280
Investigators were also struggling to figure out&nbsp;
how the fire had started in the first place -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:45.420
there was no source of flame nearby, nor a trace&nbsp;
of accelerant amidst the debris. Stranger still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:51.360
a foul-smelling oily residue coated the walls&nbsp;
and ceilings of the entire ground floor.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:57.660
To put it mildly, the entire incident was&nbsp;
a mystery. A mystery that deepened when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:02.940
the coroner declared that Michael had&nbsp;
died from spontaneous human combustion.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.240
In other words, Michael Faherty hadn't been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.800
the tragic victim of a house&nbsp;
fire - he was the house fire.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.060
Strange though it was, Michael's&nbsp;
death is far from unique - cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.720
of spontaneous human combustion&nbsp;
- people bursting into flames&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.800
without an external source of ignition&nbsp;
- have been chrocicled for centuries.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:48.840
One of the first was in 1731 when Italian Countess&nbsp;
Cornelia Bandi, went to bed feeling unwell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.680
The following morning her bedchamber was covered&nbsp;
in an oily residue and a pile of ashes was found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:01.740
by the bed. The noblewoman's body had been almost&nbsp;
entirely devoured by what must have been a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:09.120
small, very concentrated fire - all that was&nbsp;
left were her lower legs and a few stray fingers.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.880
One of the most famous victims of spontaneous&nbsp;
human combustion is Mary Reeser, a 67-year old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:22.080
Florida resident, whose apartment became&nbsp;
her funeral pyre in 1951. Her landlady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.240
noticed that the door handle was hot to the&nbsp;
touch and discovered Mary’s charred remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:34.080
inside. Only her left foot - still wearing&nbsp;
a slipper - survived the blaze. Once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.680
the walls and ceilings were&nbsp;
covered in a smelly oily residue.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.840
These are just a couple of examples among&nbsp;
hundreds of strangely similar cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.720
all sharing a few key details:

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:51.180
1. The victim's bodies are almost entirely&nbsp;
destroyed by the fire, even the bones&nbsp;

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:57.360
2. The lower legs and feet usually survive&nbsp;
completely unharmed, and are often still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.880
wearing unburnt clothing or shoes
3. A mysterious oily residue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:08.580
is found on surfaces nearby
4. And the unusually localised fire that caused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:16.140
the damage appears to have no external source: it&nbsp;
would seem the victims just... burst into flames.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.020
So, what the hell is going on&nbsp;
here? We still don’t know for sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:27.180
but some have blamed excessive alcoholism&nbsp;
whilst others have deemed it to be divine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:32.460
retribution dished out to sinners. That was the&nbsp;
angle Charles Dickens went with when he killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:38.400
off alcoholic rag and bone man and all round&nbsp;
arsehole Mr. Krook in his novel Bleak House.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:45.300
Other theories include poltergeist activity, a&nbsp;
buildup of static electricity, ball lightning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:52.260
the effects of an undiscovered subatomic&nbsp;
particle tentatively titled the pyrotron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:58.140
or possibly just holding in your farts&nbsp;
(you can thank South Park for that one).

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:03.540
There is of course another school of&nbsp;
thought - that the entire idea human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:09.000
beings can just burst into flames&nbsp;
at random is complete horsepiss.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.160
As for who's right... well, the answer&nbsp;
to that question might just surprise you.&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:21.600
Let’s start by highlighting the obvious, we know&nbsp;
humans can combust because we burn millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:28.080
them every year... in crematoriums. It’s just&nbsp;
the spontaneous part that’s controversial.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.840
We also know spontaneous combustion&nbsp;
in general is possible. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.780
exothermic reactions in large piles of compost&nbsp;
or hay can generate enough heat to ignite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.920
and the same can be said of&nbsp;
coal, charcoal, and cotton.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:51.480
So, humans burn and sometimes things randomly&nbsp;
catch on fire - is spontaneous human combustion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:59.220
really such a stretch? Actually, yeah it kind&nbsp;
of is. As of today, there is quite simply no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:05.700
proven process by which a human body - which is&nbsp;
about 60% water - could suddenly catch on fire.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.160
For that reason, the vast majority of scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:12.300
discount the notion of truly&nbsp;
“spontaneous” human combustion.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:18.120
A 1984 study of 30 historical cases&nbsp;
found that almost all the bodies were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:23.160
discovered near some kind of flame -&nbsp;
an open fire, a candle, a lit pipe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.580
that sort of thing. They also found&nbsp;
that many of the victims were smokers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:31.740
and almost all were elderly, had serious&nbsp;
health conditions, or were alcoholics.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:38.220
The conclusion was pretty simple -&nbsp;
a stray ember from a nearby fire or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:43.800
dropped cigarette lands in the victim's&nbsp;
lap, and they're either too old, too ill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.580
or too drunk to react before&nbsp;
the fire gets out of hand.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:54.120
Take Mary Reeser as a prime example - she was&nbsp;
a heavy smoker who took sleeping pills. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:57.960
spontaneous human combustion can't be ruled&nbsp;
out, it’s much more likely that she fell into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:03.300
a pill-induced sleep, whilst holding a lit&nbsp;
cigarette and thermodynamics did the rest.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:10.380
It's also a bit bloody suspicious that the vast&nbsp;
majority of victims 'spontaneously combust' when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:16.320
they're home alone without any witnesses. In the&nbsp;
age of the smartphone and Big Brother-style CCTV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.820
if people really could just catch on&nbsp;
fire for no reason you'd expect to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:25.080
see some decent footage. You've Been Framed&nbsp;
would pay good money for that kind of thing.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:31.800
Proponents of spontaneous human combustion&nbsp;
frequently cite incidents where no source of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:38.460
fire was found nearby. But as Carl Sagan&nbsp;
once said - absence of evidence does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:43.500
mean evidence of absence. In other words, just&nbsp;
because an external source of ignition isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:47.700
found near the body, it doesn't mean there&nbsp;
wasn't one to begin with - whatever sparked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:51.240
the fire could have been completely&nbsp;
consumed by the subsequent flames.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.580
OK, so… case closed, right?&nbsp;
It’s all just a myth... well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:00.720
let's not get ahead of ourselves.&nbsp;
Because even if the 'spontaneous'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:07.560
part is starting to look a little sus, what&nbsp;
happens next is still utterly baffling.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.580
As we've seen, the remains&nbsp;
left behind in these cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:18.240
are very distinctive - the torso is utterly&nbsp;
destroyed, while the legs or feet survive.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:26.340
Crematorium furnaces reach temperatures&nbsp;
approaching 1000 degrees celsius for roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:32.520
90 minutes to incinerate your average human&nbsp;
- using a tremendous amount of fuel in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:38.220
process. Even then, fragments of bone remain&nbsp;
that have to be mechanically pulverised.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:45.120
So here's the million dollar question: how&nbsp;
could a mere cigarette or errant ember falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:52.080
onto someone's lap achieve similar results&nbsp;
to an industrial furnace? And how can a fire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:57.540
that's hot enough to turn bones to ash leave&nbsp;
other parts of the body completely untouched?

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:03.360
Not only is this stuff seriously weird, it's&nbsp;
also the exact opposite of what we typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:10.080
see with victims of house fires. Normally the&nbsp;
limbs go firstand the torso tends to survive.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:13.800
For a long time, these twin enigmas remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:20.100
unexplained until an unexpected source&nbsp;
provided a clue - the humble candle.

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:26.100
Everyone knows how candles work - the wax provides&nbsp;
a steady flow of fuel, which is delivered to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:31.860
flame by way of the wick, resulting in a&nbsp;
slow, even burn that can last for hours.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:37.020
Something similar could be happening&nbsp;
with spontaneous human combustion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:42.840
A spark ignites a victim's clothing,&nbsp;
which acts like the wick. And then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:48.900
wax - aka fuel - is supplied by human&nbsp;
body fat, which is highly flammable.

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:55.740
If a small clothing fire is left unchecked because&nbsp;
the victim is incapacitated (or dead from natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.740 --> 00:10:02.940
causes), the skin beneath can rupture, allowing&nbsp;
melting body fat to seep into the clothing. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:09.180
fat fuels the fire, which melts more fat,&nbsp;
and perpetuates the cycle until there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:14.220
no more fuel to burn. In effect,&nbsp;
the victim becomes a human candle.

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:20.280
This gruesome process is known as the&nbsp;
Wick Effect, and it illuminates a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:26.100
of the weirdness we’ve seen so far. A small,&nbsp;
intense fire with a low flame that won’t easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:31.860
spread beyond its source of fuel seems like a&nbsp;
perfect fit for spontaneous human combustion.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:36.360
The distinctive destruction of the torso&nbsp;
also starts to make sense - that's where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:41.280
we carry most of our body fat. The legs survive&nbsp;
partly because they're below the fire where it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:46.800
considerably cooler, and partly because they&nbsp;
don't contain enough fat to fuel the flames.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:53.820
The wick effect also explains where that odd&nbsp;
brown residue comes from - gruesomely enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:56.340
it's just partially burnt body fat.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:59.640
But as good as the wick effect appears to be in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:04.200
explaining all the oddities of these&nbsp;
cases, there is one small problem.

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:10.380
In 2016, BBC's Earth Lab conducted an&nbsp;
experiment to demonstrate the efficacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:15.180
of the wick effect on a human body.&nbsp;
But things didn't proceed as planned.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:20.100
They wrapped a dead pig in a blanket,&nbsp;
and dropped a cigarette onto the fabric.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:25.620
A fire began to smoulder almost immediately,&nbsp;
but it proved too small to melt the body fat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:31.320
below. In the end, the team had to intervene&nbsp;
by dousing the fire in a liquid accelerant and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:36.900
actively fanning the flames - two things that most&nbsp;
certainly would *not* happen in the real world.

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:41.160
The wick effect did eventually&nbsp;
kick in, creating a small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:45.780
self-sustaining fire focused on the torso,&nbsp;
which burned for six hours straight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:51.300
The pattern of burning was shockingly&nbsp;
similar to spontaneous human combustion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:58.260
but the damage was far less severe - the 'pig&nbsp;
in blanket' was nicely charred and would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:03.360
been lovely with your Christmas turkey, but it&nbsp;
was nowhere near turning into a pile of ash.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:09.300
Now, it may simply be that the experiment wasn't&nbsp;
close enough to the real thing - a pig is a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:15.600
good substitute for a human, but it isn't perfect.&nbsp;
Nevertheless, it did cast doubts - doubts which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:21.000
only grow when you explore some of the more&nbsp;
extreme cases of spontaneous human combustion.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:27.660
On a September evening in 1982, Jeannie Saffin was&nbsp;
sitting at the kitchen table of her parent's house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:34.560
in north London with her father and brother-in-law&nbsp;
when she suddenly caught fire. According to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:39.660
brother-in-law, Jeannie was engulfed by a raging&nbsp;
inferno within seconds - there were even flames&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:45.300
shooting out of her mouth, Smaug-style.&nbsp;
She died in hospital not long afterwards.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:52.320
In another case, 5 years later, what remained&nbsp;
of Barry Soudain was found on his kitchen floor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:57.120
in Folkestone, Kent. As we’ve seen before,&nbsp;
Barry was but a big pile of ash with a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:01.380
of feet sticking out. It should have been a&nbsp;
textbook case of the wick effect in action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:07.380
except for one small problem - bubbling&nbsp;
away on the hob nearby was a pan of water.

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:13.800
It’s thought the wick effect requires roughly&nbsp;
12 hours to reduce a human to ash. But if Barry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:18.960
had been burning away for half a day or more,&nbsp;
why hadn't the pot on the stove boiled dry?

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:26.640
Other victims appear to have been fully&nbsp;
conscious when meeting their spontaneously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:33.660
fiery fate. Homeless man Robert Bailey was found&nbsp;
in an abandoned building in London in 1967. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:39.000
still-burning body was bent in agony at the foot&nbsp;
of a flight of stairs, and it appered he’d been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:43.920
writhing around before succumbing to the flames.&nbsp;
His pain had been so great that he'd bitten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:49.140
into a solid wooden bannister so forcefully&nbsp;
that Firefighters had to pry his jaw open..

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:53.460
It doesn’t take forensic science to suss that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:58.380
the wick effect alone cannot explain&nbsp;
these cases.. So, what are we missing?

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:01.140
According to research biologist Brian Ford,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:07.080
the final piece of this macabre puzzle&nbsp;
is, of all things, nail polish remover.

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:11.760
Ford was never convinced the wick effect&nbsp;
alone could turn a non-flammable human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:16.020
into a pile of ash overnight, so he tried&nbsp;
to come up with feasible scenarios that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:20.760
could increase our body’s flammability.&nbsp;
And it turns out he actually found one.

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.760
When the human body is starved of carbohydrates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:28.380
it begins to burn stored fat instead.&nbsp;
The resulting metabolic state is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:33.180
Ketosis because it results in elevated levels&nbsp;
of ketone bodies in the bloodstream and urine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:38.220
one of which is acetone - the key&nbsp;
ingredient in nail polish remover.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:41.220
Acetone is extremely flammable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:46.380
and can accumulate in fatty tissues&nbsp;
at high concentrations during ketosis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:53.100
Ford believes that, under the right circumstances,&nbsp;
a person in ketosis may become flammable.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:59.640
To test his theory, he made a bunch of tiny model&nbsp;
humans out of pork meat marinated overnight in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:06.960
acetone - as you do. He dressed these mini&nbsp;
meat men in tiny clothes, then set them on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:14.880
fire - every single one of them burned to ash in&nbsp;
under 30 minutes, leaving only their limbs behind.

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:21.300
Notably, Ford's pork soldiers burned with&nbsp;
a bright blue flame. Most spontaneous human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:27.120
combustions happen behind closed doors, but the&nbsp;
few folks who have witnessed one almost always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:33.900
mention blue flames. Incidentally, the low flames&nbsp;
produced by the wick effect are invariably orange.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:40.080
It's pretty compelling evidence, but once again&nbsp;
questions remain. Ketosis isn't an elusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:46.620
metabolic state - most of us experience it on a&nbsp;
fairly regular basis: after a session at the gym,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:51.720
when we're ill, or when we haven't eaten&nbsp;
for a while. Plenty of people enter ketosis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:57.180
on purpose in an attempt to burn fat and&nbsp;
lose weight - it’s called the Keto diet.

00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:04.080
So, if ketosis really does make the human body&nbsp;
flammable, why isn't some poor soul going up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:09.540
like a firework every five minutes? For now,&nbsp;
we don't have a good answer to that question.

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:13.560
If you're starting to spot a pattern, I'm&nbsp;
right there with you - there simply isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:18.600
enough evidence to either confirm or debunk&nbsp;
any of these theories. Cases of spontaneous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:23.640
human combustion are incredibly rare, and&nbsp;
since burning old people is apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:28.560
considered unethical, it's difficult to&nbsp;
create meaningful experiments in the lab.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:33.420
We can probably rule out the more&nbsp;
extreme theories, but there isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:37.440
really enough evidence to definitively&nbsp;
confirm the more sensible ones either.

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:42.300
So yeah, all I can say is,&nbsp;
until we meet again, please,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:45.960
please try really hard not to burst into flames.

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:47.880
Thanks for watching.

